{"attempt_id": "attempt:um-ep-790-erd-s-problem-790-da25e6ac-source-fidelity-01", "created_at": "2026-09-06T08:00:00Z", "graph_id": "graph:um-ep-790-erd-s-problem-790-da25e6ac-v1", "obligations": [{"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof", "counterexample", "formalization", "computation"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": ["obligation:um-ep-790-erd-s-problem-790-da25e6ac-statement-fidelity"], "kind": "root_claim", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-ep-790-erd-s-problem-790-da25e6ac-root", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/EP-790"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Let $l(n)$ be maximal such that if $A\\subset\\mathbb{Z}$ with $\\lvert A\\rvert=n$ then there exists a sum-free $B\\subseteq A$ with $\\lvert B\\rvert \\geq l(n)$ - that is, $B$ is such that there are no solutions to $ a_1=a_2+\\cdots+a_r $ with $a_i\\in B$ all distinct. Estimate $l(n)$. In particular, is it true that $l(n)n^{-1/2}\\to \\infty$? Is it true that $l(n)< n^{1-c}$ for some $c>0$?"}, "statement_sha256": "a62e1a8f9072787d308b0cdc300f0422a67578b92b46f07dd30c4bf95d7397a9"}, {"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": [], "kind": "definition", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-ep-790-erd-s-problem-790-da25e6ac-statement-fidelity", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/EP-790"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Verify against the cited source that the frozen statement is complete, current, untruncated, attribution-correct, and unambiguous enough for mathematical research; record any definition or quantifier gap without silently editing the contract."}, "statement_sha256": "65f0b3d574bd6d837403149dab6434c0f571709f7eb5bb066c7e8774bad13f34"}], "problem_contract_sha256": "5518fdcbd2c2fc800b5a0db4fcf07be7c631acd85fda0f58dffe74c4c02cdcc9", "prob